{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794174657,"nonce":"Lyxv8UO3vtSn45rGPhGha4PFePE=","message":"## Decisions made in building this, beyond the design above\n\nIncluding the widened scope (types and fields archived like values; \"introduce\" defined by the user):\n\n1. **A value's archive is a member of its `values/\u003cid\u003e` attribute**, `{…, \"archived\": true}`, not an entity and not a value removed. A type's or a field's is the entity's existing archived flag (`SetArchived`). Removing the value would lose its ordinal, category and name, and the issues holding it would read an unknown value.\n2. **The compiled schema keeps archived types and fields, marked.** `SchemaEntries` returns, per key, the unarchived winner, else the archived winner (same oldest-first rule). Without this an issue of an archived type could not be edited at all (\"type not in the schema\"), which contradicts the user's definition of \"introduce\". `TypeKeys`, `FieldKeys`, `ValueIds` and `ValuesInCategory` list only live entries (pickers, quickstart, refusals, the Jira `canceled` pick). `AllTypeKeys` and `AllFieldKeys` include archived ones (export, `$schema`, group order).\n3. **\"Introduce\" is enforced through `Checker.Held`.** The cache fills it from the issue's snapshot. A write is accepted when the value or item it writes is one the issue already holds. A remove never introduces. `null` clears an archived field. So: same-value set ok; list set keeping an archived member ok; adding one refused; editing other fields of an issue of an archived type ok; retype out ok, retype into refused (type's values carry the archive). Refusals name the live alternatives (`\"x\" is archived; valid values: …`, `the field is archived on task; its fields: …`).\n4. **An archive is policy**, so the shape check (Jira pull) never refuses one; a pulled archived value is reported `off_schema` like any other policy miss.\n5. **The document states an archive; it never implies one.** `archived` on a type, field or value is `true` (archive), `false` (unarchive) or absent (leave as is). Export writes `true` only, so `export | import` is a no-op and **an older `schema.yaml` imported after a sync's archive revives nothing**. Reconcile now matches a key's archived entity instead of creating a second one, `--prune` leaves already-archived entities alone, and a stated archive lands in the same commit as that entity's other changes. The one exception is a new entity stated archived: its create and its archive are two commits.\n6. **No new command form.** `schema archive KEY` stays type/field (it names an entity; a value is a member of one). A value is archived by the document. There is no `schema unarchive`: unarchiving is `archived: false` in an import. Docs and `--help` say so.\n7. **Derive's rules.** It archives on a dead Jira alias for an issue type absent from the project's types, and for a value absent from its field's discovered values (statuses per workflow, priorities, option `allowedValues`). It archives a field only when Jira has no such field at all: a custom field absent from `/field`, or a link type absent from `/issueLinkType`, but never while linking is disabled (no types listed). Fixed-table fields are never archived. A field merely off one create screen keeps the `dead alias` warning. It unarchives what is matched by alias again, whoever archived it. An archived entity/value with no Jira alias is never adopted by name; values aliased `\"\"` or not aliased to Jira are never touched.\n8. **Notes.** The archiving run warns once (`archived: …`). Later runs say it at `info`. Unarchive is `info`. `Note.Archived` carries a structured ref `{type, field?, value?}`, and the host appends `; still held by \u003chuman ids\u003e` for unarchived issues of the type / holding the field / holding the value. **A sync computes this after its pull**, so an issue Jira moved off what it removed is not named. `jira schema` has no pull and lists holders as they stand. A dry run lists them before (no) pull.\n9. **TUI.** The value picker offers live values only. A board column for an archived value (or a value of an archived field/type) is drawn only while a card is in it, in its schema place. `empty_groups` never adds its lane. A matrix axis includes it only where the data has it. Group order keeps archived values' places.\n10. **Fake Jira gained `RemoveStatus`.** It drops transitions into the status and removes it from sources; it does not migrate issues. The end-to-end test removes \"Ready for QA\" from Task.\n11. **Changed tests.** `cache` `TestSchemaEntriesSkipArchived` is replaced by `TestArchiveRefusesOnlyWhatIsNew` (an archived field is now compiled, marked). The schema reconcile test helper and the jira derive test store keep archived entries marked rather than dropping them.\n\nDocs: `doc/design/schema.md` gains \"Archiving refuses only what is new\" and \"An archive is stated, never implied\" (plus edits to \"What a type and a field carry\", \"Validate on write, lenient on read\", \"Compiled on demand\", the document's key list, \"Import is an upsert\", \"Archive and rm\"). `doc/design/jira.md` gains \"What Jira drops is archived\" and rewrites \"Derive, then the schema import\"'s contract and \"A pull is checked for shape, not policy\"'s policy list. `doc/design/views.md`'s columns, empty swimlanes and axis order mention archived values. `host/quickstart.md` and `schema archive --help` describe the archive.","files":null}]}